  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Let $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\u27f6[\\begin{aligned}-2x+y \\\\ -x+4y \\\\ -3x-5y\\end{aligned}]$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$",
  "worked_solution": "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2x+y \\\\ -x+4y \\\\ -3x-5y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y=6 \\\\ -x+4y=-4 \\\\ -3x-5y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+4y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =6 \\\\ -2(4+4y)+y & =6 \\\\ -7y & =14 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+4y \\\\  & =4+4(-2) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-2$ into the third equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x-5y & =k \\\\ -3(-4)-5(-2) & =k \\\\ k & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=22.$",
